How about declaring all the arrays (a, b, a64, b64, a128, b128) as
"volatile"? This way the compiler should not be able to optimize
anything away (eg.  precompute the results of the multiplications).


Ordinary run of the mill x86 hardware *may* emit fma SIMD asm : 

     vfmadd213ss     %xmm2, %xmm0, %xmm1    

However no promise that doesn't throw a SIGILL.


On a 32-bit VIA Eden processor type machine running FreeBSD 13.2-RELEASE 
we may use CFLAGS : 

    CFLAGS= -std=iso9899:1999 -m32 -g -O0 -fno-builtin -fno-fast-math -pedantic -pedantic-errors -Weverything -Wno-reserved-id-macro -ffp-contract=on -mfma


In this case we do get the SIMD opcodes and that results in SIGILL.


